      "msg_06b3b81688bcc6c0016adce51d4b48819085dcaad577ccb513": {
        "id": "msg_06b3b81688bcc6c0016adce51d4b48819085dcaad577ccb513",
        "message": {
          "id": "msg_06b3b81688bcc6c0016adce51d4b48819085dcaad577ccb513",
          "author": {
            "role": "assistant",
            "name": null,
            "metadata": {}
          },
          "create_time": 1792861469.32,
          "update_time": 1792861473.129,
          "content": {
            "content_type": "text",
            "parts": [
              "Yes, that is a good intuition. Smaller \\(\\epsilon\\) concentrates the density more tightly around the zeros, making nearby distinct zeros easier to distinguish; larger \\(\\epsilon\\) broadens their density profiles.\n\nThe useful qualification is that **\\(\\epsilon\\) sets a scale for the function value \\(|H|\\)**. The corresponding spatial resolution depends on how quickly \\(H\\) changes. Near a simple zero \\(a\\),\n\\[\nH(t,z)\\approx H_z(t,a)(z-a),\n\\qquad\n\\text{characteristic width}\\approx\\frac{\\epsilon}{|H_z(t,a)|}.\n\\]\nThus the same \\(\\epsilon\\) can give different spatial widths at different zeros.\n\nNear an order-\\(m\\) zero, \\(H\\approx c(z-a)^m\\), the width scales as \\((\\epsilon/|c|)^{1/m}\\). As \\(\\epsilon\\to0\\), the density concentrates into the point masses in the equation, retaining each zero’s multiplicity as its limiting mass."
            ]
          },
          "status": "finished_successfully",
          "end_turn": true,
          "metadata": {
            "source": "Codex completed public message",
            "source_turn_id": "01a16ede-b0b3-73c0-b7c4-ce85fcb6fea4",
            "phase": "final_answer",
            "created_at_utc": "2026-10-24T17:04:29.320Z",
            "completed_at_utc": "2026-10-24T17:04:33.129Z",
            "created_at_local": "2026-10-24T13:04:29.320-04:00"
          },
          "recipient": "all"
        },
        "parent": "msg_06b3b81688bcc6c0016adce50bf12c8190a7284ecce6c0887e",
